<h2>What a preventive visit includes</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>A thorough exam — teeth, gums, bite, soft tissues, and oral cancer screening</li>
  <li>Digital X-rays (low radiation) when clinically indicated, typically once a year</li>
  <li>Professional cleaning to remove plaque and calculus your toothbrush can't reach</li>
  <li>Polishing to remove surface stain</li>
  <li>Fluoride treatment when helpful</li>
  <li>A clear summary of what we found and what (if anything) we recommend next</li>
</ul>

<h2>How often should you come in?</h2>
<p>Most healthy adults do well on a six-month recall. Patients with a history of gum disease, cavity risk, or specific medical conditions may benefit from a three- or four-month interval. We'll explain our reasoning and you have the final say.</p>

<h2>Sealants and fluoride</h2>
<p>Dental sealants are thin protective coatings painted onto the chewing surfaces of back teeth, where most cavities start. Combined with regular fluoride treatments and good home care, sealants are a simple, effective way to lower cavity risk for patients who are prone to decay.</p>

<h2>Oral cancer screening</h2>
<p>Every routine exam includes a soft-tissue exam — checking your tongue, throat, cheeks, and lips for early signs of oral cancer. Most patients never need anything more than the exam itself; if something does need a closer look, we'll explain why and what comes next.</p>

<h2>Why preventive visits matter</h2>
<p>Cavities and gum disease rarely hurt in their early stages. By the time a tooth aches, the problem is usually bigger and costs more to fix. A cleaning and exam every six months lets us catch a small cavity while it needs only a small <a href="/services/dental-fillings/">filling</a>, and spot early <a href="/services/gum-disease-treatment/">gum disease</a> while it can still be reversed.</p>
<p>Your mouth also tells us about your general health. Bleeding gums, dry mouth, worn teeth or sores can point to diabetes, medication side effects, grinding or acid reflux, and we'll tell you when something is worth mentioning to your physician.</p>

<h2>Your first preventive visit with us</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>We review your health history and any concerns you have</li>
  <li>Full-mouth digital X-rays and a comprehensive exam with Dr. Phillips</li>
